INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - MORNING

A long scrubbed-pine table. Sunlight through gingham curtains. A cast-iron stove ticks as it warms. Four people, four chairs, one pot of oatmeal between them.

GRANDMOTHER AGATHA TSVETKOVA-BELL (70s, apron, silver bun) ladles porridge. At her left sits her son-in-law DAMIEN OKAFOR-LINDSAY (40s, work shirt, reading the paper). Across from him, his wife NOELLE (40s, hair still wet from the shower) butters toast. And at the end, in a chair too big for him, sits a quiet BOY (8, striped pajamas) who has not touched his bowl.

                    AGATHA
          Eat, child.

                    BOY
          Not hungry.

                    NOELLE
          Just a little.

                    BOY ^
          Not hungry.

The two voices overlap, hers soft and his small. Damien lowers the paper.

                    DAMIEN
          Let him be.

                    AGATHA
          He was up all night. I heard him on the stairs.

                    NOELLE
          Was he sleepwalking?

                    AGATHA
          He was talking.

Damien turns a page. Noelle sets the knife down. The Boy stirs his oatmeal with one finger.

                    NOELLE
          Who were you talking to, sweetheart?

                    BOY
          The man.

                    DAMIEN
          What man?

                    BOY
          The one at the end of the hall. He said you'd be angry.

Agatha's ladle stops in the air. Damien sets the paper on the table. Noelle looks at the stove, and then at her mother.

                    AGATHA
          (to the table)
          Eat your breakfast.

She resumes ladling, and nobody speaks again.
